+	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two Three") == 0);
+	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two\r\n Three") == -1);
 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One\r", "Two") == -1);
 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One\n", "Two") == -1);
 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two\r") == -1);
Description: CVE-2026-63384 - Fix integer overflow in evtag_unmarshal_header
 On platforms where int is 32 bits, if evtag_unmarshal_header() tried to
 decode a header declaring a payload longer than INT_MAX bytes, the
 attacker-controlled uint32 length decoded by evtag_decode_int() would wrap
 into a negative signed int on return. Callers could then perform incorrect
 behaviors, incorrect/oversized allocations, or trigger a denial-of-service.
 .
 This is fixed by rejecting any decoded payload length larger than INT_MAX
 in evtag_unmarshal_header(). <limits.h> (providing INT_MAX) is already
 included by event_tagging.c.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/109c16499282959d70f56ec3baf4c8b1e6646bda
Author: Nick Mathewson <nickm@torproject.org>
Bug: GHSA-45c6-qx49-89m8
Last-Update: 2026-08-22
---
--- a/event_tagging.c
+++ b/event_tagging.c
@@ -445,7 +445,7 @@ evtag_unmarshal_header(struct evbuffer *evbuf, ev_uint32_t *ptag)

 	if (decode_tag_internal(ptag, evbuf, 1 /* dodrain */) == -1)
 		return (-1);
-	if (evtag_decode_int(&len, evbuf) == -1)
+	if (evtag_decode_int(&len, evbuf) == -1 || len > INT_MAX)
 		return (-1);

 	if (evbuffer_get_length(evbuf) < len)
--- a/include/event2/tag.h
+++ b/include/event2/tag.h
@@ -64,6 +64,8 @@ void evtag_init(void);
 /**
    Unmarshals the header and returns the length of the payload

+   Returns an error if the payload length is above INT_MAX.
+
    @param evbuf the buffer from which to unmarshal data
    @param ptag a pointer in which the tag id is being stored
    @returns -1 on failure or the number of bytes in the remaining payload.
Description: CVE-2026-63387 - Fix out-of-bounds write in dnsname_to_labels
 dnsname_to_labels() would write a single terminating 0 byte off the end of
 the destination stack buffer if the code ever tried to make a DNS response
 exactly 2^16 + 1 bytes long, ending with a DNS name. This one-byte
 out-of-bounds write is prevented by checking that the write offset is still
 within the buffer before appending the terminating 0 byte, returning -2
 (overflow) otherwise. Resolves GHSA-58rx-7448-jw47.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/377b9022c3ac61aa4540b5dc4b70c60bf74c663d
Author: Nick Mathewson <nickm@torproject.org>
Last-Update: 2026-08-22
---
--- a/evdns.c
+++ b/evdns.c
@@ -1671,7 +1671,10 @@ dnsname_to_labels(u8 *const buf, size_t buf_len, off_t j,
 	/* the labels must be terminated by a 0. */
 	/* It's possible that the name ended in a . */
 	/* in which case the zero is already there */
-	if (!j || buf[j-1]) buf[j++] = 0;
+	if ((size_t)j >= buf_len)
+		return -2;
+	if (!j || buf[j-1])
+		buf[j++] = 0;
 	return j;
  overflow:
 	return (-2);
Description: CVE-2026-63388 - bufferevent: fix heap out-of-bounds write via AF_UNIX+http+NDEBUG
 When running an evhttp server over an AF_UNIX socket on a copy of libevent
 compiled with NDEBUG, an attacker could use a crafted AF_UNIX address to cause
 a heap overflow when the connection address was recorded via
 bufferevent_socket_set_conn_address_(). The internal conn_address field was an
 undersized union (sockaddr_in/sockaddr_in6) and the only length guard was an
 NDEBUG-gated EVUTIL_ASSERT, so an over-long address (such as a sockaddr_un)
 overflowed the heap buffer (or aborted when built without NDEBUG).
 .
 The fix:
  - stores conn_address as a struct sockaddr_storage large enough to hold an
    AF_UNIX address;
  - checks the provided address length unconditionally (regardless of NDEBUG)
    and returns EVUTIL_EAI_FAIL instead of copying when it is too long;
  - propagates that failure through bufferevent_socket_set_conn_address_()'s
    new int return value in the getaddrinfo callback and in
    evhttp_get_request_connection().
 .
 A regression test (bufferevent_set_conn_address_overflow) is added to
 test/regress_bufferevent.c to exercise the unconditional length check.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/52057cb33d0c20c0a0453fbabe6c0c96854931b9
